    <item rdf:about="http://alephnull.com/w/linux/serial?rev=1221404198&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2008-09-14T10:56:38-05:00</dc:date>
        <title>linux:serial</title>
        <link>http://alephnull.com/w/linux/serial?rev=1221404198&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Ethernet


The house ethernet standard is T568B:

 RJ45 Pin  Color         Pair  1         White/orange  2  2         Orange        2  3         White/Green   3  4         Blue          1  5         White/Blue    1  6         Green         3  7         White/Brown   4  8         Brown         4 
Serial

NULL


This mapping is the same for male and female connectors.</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="http://alephnull.com/w/linux/start?rev=1245752483&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2009-06-23T06:21:23-05:00</dc:date>
        <title>linux:start</title>
        <link>http://alephnull.com/w/linux/start?rev=1245752483&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>*  Summary of my  Linux Work
	*  C code for printing a symbolic stack  backtrace inside a program (i.e., at runtime)
		*  Download  backtrace.c
		*  See also HP's  libunwind

	*   Linux Software Development and Performance Analysis Tools
	*   The ur library
	*   The Compaq Evo T30
	*   The Dell Axim X5
	*   Serial Cable Pinouts
	*  Other Pinouts
		*  &lt;http://www.allpinouts.com/&gt;
		*  &lt;http://www.hardwarebook.info/&gt;
		*  &lt;http://pinouts.ru/&gt;</description>
    </item>
